          { name: "Treating the 66% Margin as a Demographic Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% Republican result in a district that is 33% Hispanic and approximately 16% AIAN is not demographic confirmation that two-thirds of the constituency is naturally Republican — it is an electoral snapshot that may reflect turnout patterns, candidate quality, or a specific political moment more than durable coalition alignment. Any messaging or governance posture that implicitly treats this as a mandate from the district's full population, rather than a strong result from a portion of it, will read as tone-deaf to the Hispanic and AIAN communities whose civic engagement can shift materially cycle to cycle. The 34% Democratic share in 2024, while a minority, represents tens of thousands of constituents whose concerns are real and whose mobilization potential is unexhausted." },
          { name: "Ethnic or Cultural Dismissiveness Toward the Hispanic Community", tag: "avoid", why: "With 32.97% Hispanic residents, AR-HD-7 cannot be governed — or campaigned — as though it is a culturally homogeneous Anglo district that happens to have a large Latino population. Any rhetoric, legislative positioning, or constituent service posture that signals indifference or hostility toward the cultural, linguistic, or economic concerns of Hispanic families will fracture the coalition that produces the current 66% margin and, more importantly, will fail the basic governance obligation to one-third of the district's residents. At $79,069 median income, many Hispanic households in this district are working-class homeowners with the same economic stakes as the Anglo majority — the issue is whether they feel genuinely represented, and candidates who answer that question poorly face a structurally motivated challenger with a clear path to 10-point gains." }
