          "The racial composition — 69.23% white, 13.2% Hispanic/Latino, 2.32% Asian, 0.91% Black, with a residual gap of roughly 14% that likely includes American Indian/Alaska Native residents — describes a district with meaningful minority communities whose turnout and preferences can shift margins in a competitive cycle. The 50% college attainment rate is the single most important number in this profile: nationally and regionally, college-educated suburban voters have proven to be the most volatile and persuadable constituency in recent elections, and a district at exactly the median boundary of college attainment is a district where a message calibrated to professional, economically secure, civically engaged adults can find genuine traction. At 35-year median age, this is a constituency of young professionals and families at the stage of life when school quality, housing affordability, and economic trajectory are front-of-mind governance concerns — not abstract ideological positioning. The current incumbent holds this seat by default; the strategic question is whether they are building the authentic constituent relationships that would hold it in a contested race."
