          { name: "AIAN and Minority Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The ~9.25% demographic gap in AR-HD-22 — after accounting for the four reported categories — likely reflects a meaningful American Indian or Alaska Native population in an Arkansas district context, potentially connected to federally recognized tribal communities or descendants of the Trail of Tears era with deep historical roots in the state. Combined with the 8.84% Hispanic/Latino and 4.52% Black populations, the district's non-white and non-reported communities together represent approximately 25% of residents — a quarter of the constituency whose electoral activation and candidate preference are entirely obscured by the 2024 unopposed result. This is not a constituency to be managed with token acknowledgment; it is a significant bloc of residents whose civic engagement, if organized, could be decisive in a contested race. The careful designation reflects that authentic engagement requires real investment and specific knowledge, not generic outreach — getting this wrong is worse than underinvestment." },
          { name: "Treating the Unopposed Result as a Measure of Popularity", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed race is a measure of organizational capacity and opposition recruitment failure — it says nothing about how constituents evaluate their representative's performance or how they would vote in a genuine contest. AR-HD-22's demographic profile — young, renter-heavy, above-average education, meaningful minority population — describes a constituency that is structurally capable of generating a competitive challenger once organized. Any public messaging, campaign posture, or legislative behavior that projects unearned confidence based on the 100% result will register to attentive constituents as disconnected from their actual experience, which is the precise posture that recruits credible opponents. The 2024 result is an opportunity to build deep relationships, not proof that deep relationships are unnecessary." },
          { name: "Generic Conservative or Liberal Ideological Framing", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with 41.3% college attainment, a 28.4 median age, and a 43.8% renter rate is not a constituency that responds to abstract ideological positioning — it is a constituency that evaluates on competence, specificity, and whether the representative understands their material circumstances. Rote partisan messaging calibrated to a different district's base — whether conservative homeowner-focused framing or progressive urban-coded messaging — will read as out of place to a young, educated, renter-heavy constituency that is asking practical questions about housing, wages, and economic opportunity. The absence of a 2024 opponent does not validate any particular message strategy; it simply means the message has not yet been tested against an alternative." }
