          "The demographic categories reported — 73.27% white, 6.72% Hispanic, 2.15% Asian, 0.88% Black — sum to approximately 83%, leaving a ~17% gap that in an Arkansas district of this geographic character almost certainly represents American Indian and Alaska Native residents. Arkansas is home to federally recognized tribal communities and a substantial population with Indigenous heritage, and a 17% unaccounted share is not statistical noise — it is a constituency. At $66,685 median income with 25.7% renters, the district has a meaningful share of cost-sensitive households for whom healthcare access, rural infrastructure, and wage growth are not abstract policy debates but material daily concerns. The absence of a 2024 challenger means the incumbent has never had to answer publicly for a legislative record, articulate a district-specific economic agenda under pressure, or demonstrate that the coalition supporting an uncontested win would hold against a credible opponent — all of which creates strategic exposure that an organized challenger could exploit in a future cycle."
