          "The 18.9% college attainment rate — well below the national average of roughly 35% — combined with a $61,238 median household income and 42.7 median age describes a constituency of working-age and older adults who built their economic lives through trades, agriculture, small business, and manufacturing rather than professional-credential careers. At this income level, the gap between reported assets and lived financial vulnerability is narrow: a major medical event, a farm income disruption, or a regional employer closure can rapidly destabilize households that appear middle-income on paper. The 17% Democratic share is not a credible opposition threat at current levels, but it is a real floor of dissatisfaction — and in a district this demographically homogeneous, that 17% almost certainly reflects specific economic grievances rather than partisan identity. Understanding what those grievances are, and being seen to address them, is the only strategic obligation that matters here."
