        issues: [
          { name: "Renter Housing Costs and Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 50.3% of residents renting — a majority — and median household income at just $45,445, housing affordability is not a peripheral issue but the central economic stress point of the district. Renters at this income level are spending a disproportionate share of income on housing and have no asset buffer against rent increases or economic shocks. A candidate who speaks specifically to rental cost pressures, tenant protections, and affordable housing development is directly addressing the material reality of the district's demographic majority in housing-tenure terms. This is one of the clearest single-issue alignments between the data and a winnable political message in the entire district profile." },
          { name: "Workforce Development and Economic Mobility", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 16.4% college attainment — a figure that places the district's educational credential level well below state and national averages — the dominant economic pathway for residents is not four-year degree employment but skilled trades, service sector work, and light industrial employment. A legislative agenda centered on vocational training, apprenticeship pipelines, community college access, and employer-linked workforce programs speaks directly to how the 83.6% of adult residents without college degrees actually build economic security. At a 33.4-year median age, these residents are in the prime workforce formation years where credential and skills investment has the highest lifetime return, making this a constituency that is actively evaluating whether state government is helping them get ahead." },
          { name: "Black and Hispanic Community Constituent Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 16.81% Black and 9.5% Hispanic — together roughly 26% of the district — these communities are substantial enough that the 68% Republican margin almost certainly reflects meaningful cross-racial support or significant turnout differentials, either of which requires active maintenance rather than passive assumption. In a lower-income, younger district, economic concerns around job access, healthcare, and housing costs cut across racial lines and provide a genuine shared-interest framework for constituent engagement. The risk is treating these communities as a monolithic bloc or assuming that existing support is locked in; any candidate who takes Black and Hispanic constituent relationships for granted in a district this economically stressed will find the coalition erodes at precisely the moment a credible challenger is making a targeted economic argument." },
          { name: "Culture-War Framing as the Primary Campaign Message", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with $45,445 median income, 50.3% renter rate, and 33.4-year median age, the constituency's primary political concerns are material — housing, wages, healthcare costs, workforce opportunity — not ideological. A campaign that leads with culture-war content rather than economic substance risks being perceived as indifferent to the actual pressures facing working-class renters in their thirties, which is the profile of the median HD-36 voter. More critically, culture-war-dominant messaging tends to depress turnout among younger voters and can alienate the Black and Hispanic communities whose support is structurally necessary to hold 68% in this demographic environment. The 68% result reflects economic coalition-building, not ideological homogeneity, and messaging that doesn't reflect that risks fracturing what built it." },
          { name: "Ignoring the ~5% AIAN Population Gap", tag: "avoid", why: "The four reported demographic categories sum to approximately 95.1%, leaving a ~4.9% gap that in Arkansas most plausibly reflects American Indian or Alaska Native residents. While smaller than the AIAN gaps in Oklahoma districts in this sequence, in a working-class district with tight resource constraints, every identifiable constituency that goes unengaged is a potential source of either opposition mobilization or turnout suppression. Arkansas has federally recognized tribal communities, and AIAN residents in this income and age bracket share the same housing, healthcare, and workforce concerns as their neighbors — being specifically seen and named in constituent engagement costs nothing and closes a gap that a challenger could otherwise exploit." }
        ],
        memoHeadline: "AR-HD-36: A 68% Republican Lock with a Young, Renter-Majority, Economically Stressed Constituency Where the Coalition Rests on Holding a Multiracial Working-Class Base",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "AR-HD-36 is a safe Republican seat by any conventional measure — 68% for Rye in 2024 is a commanding margin — but the demographic profile underneath that result tells a more complicated story about the kind of constituency that delivered it. At $45,445 median household income, 16.4% college attainment, and a 50.3% renter rate, this is a working-class district where economic precarity is not a fringe condition but the median reality. The 33.4-year median age signals a younger-than-average constituency that has not yet accumulated the asset wealth associated with stable conservative political alignment — renters at this income level are acutely exposed to cost-of-living pressures, and their political loyalty is transactional in ways that homeowner-majority constituencies' loyalty is not. A candidate who holds 68% in this district is doing so by delivering, or being perceived as delivering, on the material concerns of people who are financially exposed.",
          "The racial composition of the district adds strategic complexity to the coalition question. At 67.7% white, 16.81% Black, 9.5% Hispanic, and 1.12% Asian, the four reported categories sum to approximately 95.1% — leaving a ~4.9% gap that likely reflects American Indian or Alaska Native residents, modest but not negligible in a state where tribal communities can have outsized local civic influence. A 68% Republican result in a district that is nearly one-third non-white requires that the Republican candidate is genuinely competitive — not merely tolerated — among Black and Hispanic voters, or that turnout differentials are doing significant work. Either way, the strategic imperative is the same: the multiracial working-class base that makes a 68% result possible in this demographic environment is not guaranteed, and it is not held by ideology alone. It is held by constituent service, economic relevance, and the perception that the representative's priorities reflect the lived pressures of people at $45,445."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Anchor the campaign in working-class economic security — at $45,445 income and 50.3% renter rate, the median constituent is financially exposed to housing costs, healthcare costs, and wage stagnation in ways that require specific policy answers, not abstract conservative positioning.",
          "Treat Black and Hispanic constituent engagement as a coalition maintenance priority, not an outreach gesture — together these communities represent roughly 26% of the district, and a 68% Republican margin in a district this diverse requires genuine support, not just opposition suppression.",
          "Lean into constituent service infrastructure: at 16.4% college attainment, district residents are more likely to need direct legislative office support navigating state agencies, benefits, and workforce programs — visibility and responsiveness here is itself a political asset.",
          "Do not mistake 68% for invulnerability — a young, renter-majority, lower-income constituency is structurally volatile if economic conditions deteriorate or if a credible challenger frames the race around cost-of-living failures; the margin is a current alignment, not a permanent structural fact."
        ]
