          { name: "Campaigning on the Unopposed Result as Evidence of Popularity", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed race in 2024 produces a 100% partisan result that reflects the absence of a challenger, not the breadth of constituent support — treating it as a mandate or a popularity signal is an analytical error with strategic consequences. The district's 35.6% renter rate, 16.9% college attainment, and $53,528 income all suggest latent economic grievances that have not yet been organized into electoral opposition, but that organization can happen quickly once a credible challenger identifies the fault lines. A candidate who campaigns on the strength of an uncontested win is signaling to potential opponents that the incumbent has not stress-tested their coalition and does not know where it is vulnerable. The strategic posture should be the opposite: govern and communicate as though a serious challenger is already doing opposition research." },
          { name: "Ignoring the ~5.8% AIAN Population Gap", tag: "avoid", why: "The four reported Census categories sum to approximately 94.2%, leaving a ~5.8% gap that in an Arkansas legislative district almost certainly represents American Indian and Alaska Native residents with deep regional and historical ties. At the district's income and population scale, a 5.8% AIAN community is numerically small but civically connected — tribal community networks in Arkansas have demonstrated the capacity to mobilize around candidates and issues when they feel their constituencies are being treated as invisible. A legislator who never acknowledges this community, never engages tribal institutions, and has no visible record of constituent service to AIAN residents is not serving the full district — and in a future competitive race, that absence becomes an opposition research target. The cost of engagement is low; the cost of sustained inattention is an organized bloc that has every reason to back a challenger." }
