          "The four reported demographic categories sum to approximately 94.2% — White at 80.45%, Hispanic/Latino at 10.97%, Black at 1.7%, and Asian at 1.07% — leaving a ~5.8% gap that in Arkansas almost certainly reflects American Indian and Alaska Native residents, potentially including citizens of the Cherokee Nation, the Choctaw Nation, or other nations with Arkansas-area historical presence. At 35.2 years median age, this is a relatively young district for a rural, low-education constituency, which suggests a working-age population that has not yet migrated out entirely — a sign of some economic anchoring, but also a constituency whose economic trajectory is not yet fixed. The 10.97% Hispanic/Latino share is the district's most significant minority community and warrants specific constituent engagement; in Arkansas legislative districts of this income and education profile, Hispanic working families are disproportionately represented in agriculture, food processing, and construction — sectors with direct policy exposure at the state level. Pearce's unopposed status is a strategic asset that must not be mistaken for invulnerability: an uncontested race produces no public data about the district's fault lines, and the first credible challenger who maps those fault lines will have an informational advantage the incumbent does not currently have."
