          { name: "Local Infrastructure and Quality of Life", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 40.2-year median age and a 30.8% renter rate together profile a district where roads, broadband, healthcare access, and housing affordability are direct daily concerns — not background political abstractions. In a modestly-income, below-average-college-attainment community, the gap between adequate and inadequate public infrastructure is felt concretely in household budgets and daily commutes. A legislator who secures visible infrastructure investments — road maintenance, broadband expansion, rural healthcare facility preservation — can point to tangible evidence of effective representation that is nearly impossible for a challenger to argue against. These wins also serve the renter community, which has fewer alternative resources to compensate for infrastructure failures than homeowners do." },
          { name: "Minority Community Constituent Service", tag: "careful", why: "The district's 6.33% Black, 4.6% Hispanic, and likely small AIAN populations together represent roughly 12-15% of residents — a share that has no electoral leverage to threaten the 70-30 margin but whose visible neglect can become a reputational liability. In a district this lopsided, constituent service quality across all community segments is the measure of governing credibility, not electoral competitiveness. A legislator who is visibly responsive to minority community concerns — even in small, specific ways — forecloses the opposition's ability to build a narrative around exclusion that could, over multiple cycles, depress base turnout among persuadable moderates. The careful framing is not about political concession but about basic constituent service that a 70% mandate actually obligates." },
          { name: "Treating the 70% Margin as an Invitation to Neglect Constituent Contact", tag: "avoid", why: "A 70-30 result can create a false sense of security that reduces an incumbent's urgency around town halls, constituent casework, and local visibility — and in a working-class district at $60,666 median income, that neglect is felt and remembered. The 30% opposition is not inert; it represents roughly three in ten voters who are paying attention and ready to amplify any perception that the representative has become comfortable and distant. In a low-college-attainment constituency, personal relationships and direct accessibility carry more political weight than policy positioning, and an incumbent who disappears between elections is vulnerable to any challenger who simply shows up. The 70% is a product of active coalition maintenance, not a structural guarantee." },
          { name: "Abstract Cultural Positioning as a Substitute for Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "At 26.8% college attainment and $60,666 median income, this district's voters are economically pragmatic — they respond to whether government is delivering on cost-of-living, jobs, and healthcare, not to the intensity of a candidate's cultural signaling. A campaign or legislative record that leads heavily with abstract cultural battles while the district's roads, broadband, and healthcare infrastructure remain unaddressed will eventually generate the kind of low-grade base dissatisfaction that either suppresses turnout or fuels a primary challenge from someone promising to actually govern. Cultural conservatism is baseline expected in this district, not a differentiator — the differentiator is whether the representative can point to concrete, local, economic wins that justify the margin." }
