          { name: "Economic Mobility for Young Working Adults", tag: "lean-into", why: "The district's 33.2-year median age means the plurality of voters are early-to-mid-career adults navigating wage growth, healthcare costs, and workforce advancement in real time. The 31.9% college attainment rate signals that a majority of working adults in the district do not hold four-year degrees, and a message built primarily around credentialed professional concerns will miss the working-class majority. Workforce training, wage floors, and healthcare access for non-credentialed workers are the material issues most directly tied to whether this constituency sees the incumbent as someone who is governing for them specifically — not for an abstract constituency that looks different from their own lives." },
          { name: "Black and Hispanic Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 16.85% Black and 8.61% Hispanic, these communities together represent roughly one in four district residents and are almost certainly load-bearing components of Magie's 54% coalition. The strategic risk is not hostility but neglect — in a district where the dominant demographic narrative centers on younger white renters, the Black and Hispanic communities can be treated as assumed rather than actively engaged. Specific constituent service, visible presence in those communities, and policy attention to the distinct economic and civic concerns of each group — rather than a unified 'minority communities' framing — is what converts assumed support into mobilized turnout when the margin is 8 points." },
          { name: "Framing Economic Issues Around Homeownership or Asset Accumulation", tag: "avoid", why: "With 48.1% of residents renting, a legislative and campaign identity built around homeownership, property values, or asset-building pathways speaks directly to less than half the district and implicitly positions the other half as outside the frame of the candidate's primary concern. At 33.2 years median age and $61,771 income, many renters in this district are not on a near-term path to homeownership, and messaging that treats ownership as the normative aspiration can feel exclusionary rather than aspirational. The risk is not offense but disconnection — and in a 54-46 district, disconnection from the renter majority is an electoral liability the candidate cannot afford." },
          { name: "Running a Base-Only Consolidation Campaign", tag: "avoid", why: "A 54-46 result means 46% of district voters actively chose a different candidate in 2024, and that opposition is not marginal — it is nearly half the electorate. In a district with a young, high-renter, racially diverse base that requires active maintenance to turn out, a strategy that focuses exclusively on consolidating existing Democratic support while ignoring what is driving the 46% will find both problems compounding simultaneously: base erosion from renter mobility and roll-off, and a hardened opposition that goes unaddressed. The winning strategy requires understanding what is generating the 46% — whether it is economic frustration, specific local governance concerns, or cultural signaling — and offering concrete responses that prevent further erosion without alienating the coalition that produced the 54%." }
