          { name: "Ideological Positioning Beyond the Constituency's Actual Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "An 85% result in a working-class, $69,547-income district reflects partisan consolidation, not ideological mandate — the coalition includes many households whose primary political motivation is economic security and local competence, not culture-war positioning. A legislator who mistakes a supermajority margin for a license to pursue aggressive ideological agendas risks alienating the pragmatic center of a coalition that currently has no alternative destination but could express dissatisfaction through abstention or primary challenge. The 21.9% college attainment rate and working-household income profile are signals of a constituency that evaluates their representative on whether government is working for them, not on ideological purity. Overreach here does not cost votes in the general election — it erodes the active enthusiasm that makes an 85% result durable rather than merely inertial." },
          { name: "Treating the 15% Democratic Vote as Irrelevant", tag: "avoid", why: "Fifteen percent in an 85-15 district is a small but real constituency — roughly one in seven voters who actively chose the alternative, in a district with only 14% renters and 92.86% white population where the Democratic vote is almost certainly composed of economic moderates, local dissidents, and working households with specific grievances rather than ideological opponents. Dismissing this constituency entirely forecloses the possibility of meaningful constituent service for a non-trivial share of the district's residents and, more importantly, sends a signal to the pragmatic center of the 85% coalition about how the legislator treats people who don't agree with them. In a district with no competitive threat, the case for inclusive constituent service is both the right governance posture and the most effective long-term strategy for keeping the supermajority ceiling from becoming a floor." }
