          "The district's 29.6% renter rate and 35.9 median age are the most strategically underappreciated numbers in this profile. Nearly a third of households are renters — a constituency whose economic concerns around housing costs, wage stagnation, and access to healthcare differ materially from the homeowner majority — and the relatively young median age means this is not an entrenched, low-turnout electorate with locked-in partisan habits. At $69,101 in median household income and 19.8% college attainment, the district is working-class by credential and middle-income by wage, a combination that produces constituencies responsive to concrete economic delivery rather than abstract ideological positioning. An incumbent who governs this seat as though the unopposed result represents organic, durable support is making the strategic error of confusing ballot access failure by the opposition with genuine constituent satisfaction — two very different conditions that a future cycle's recruited candidate could expose quickly."
