          { name: "Rural Infrastructure & Healthcare Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 40.9 median age in a low-college-attainment, majority-homeowner district means a constituency that is aging into greater healthcare utilization just as rural healthcare infrastructure faces sustained pressure across Arkansas. At $57,773 household income, out-of-pocket healthcare costs and the distance to viable medical facilities are not abstract — they are direct financial and logistical burdens on real families. Rural road and broadband infrastructure similarly affects both quality of life and economic competitiveness for a district whose workforce is largely place-bound rather than mobile. Naming these issues with specificity — not just invoking 'rural communities' generically — is what distinguishes a legislator with genuine constituent knowledge from one coasting on an uncontested ballot line." },
          { name: "AIAN Constituent Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported demographic categories sum to approximately 95.55%, leaving a ~4.64% gap that in Arkansas context most plausibly reflects American Indian or Alaska Native residents — a smaller share than in Oklahoma's tribal districts but still a real constituency with specific needs. Healthcare access through Indian Health Service, economic opportunity in rural communities, and cultural recognition are the primary concerns this community brings to legislative relationships. A candidate who acknowledges this constituency explicitly — rather than treating the 81.85% white majority as the entirety of the district — builds both governing credibility and a durable relationship with a community that notices when it is invisible. The care required is in doing this authentically and with specific knowledge of the relevant tribal nations and communities present in the district, rather than with generic minority-outreach language." },
          { name: "Treating the Unopposed Result as Evidence of Invulnerability", tag: "avoid", why: "Running unopposed in 2024 produces a 100% Republican result that is arithmetically meaningless as a measure of underlying support — it reflects opponent recruitment failure, not constituent enthusiasm. A candidate who interprets the absence of opposition as validation of their legislative record or constituent relationships is drawing a false inference from a data-free result. In a district with real working-class economic pressures at $57,773 income and 16.0% college attainment, unaddressed constituent frustrations can accumulate beneath the surface of an uncontested cycle and crystallize rapidly when a credible challenger emerges. The strategic error is mistaking silence for satisfaction." },
          { name: "Professional-Class or Credential-Centric Policy Framing", tag: "avoid", why: "At 16.0% college attainment — a figure that places the district well below most state averages — a policy agenda framed around four-year degree pathways, professional licensing reform, or knowledge-economy development will resonate with a small minority of constituents while implicitly signaling indifference to the majority. The 84% of adults without college degrees in AR-HD-60 are not a constituency that responds to credential-centric economic language; they are a constituency that evaluates whether their legislator understands the actual texture of working life in a rural, trade-and-service economy. Framing economic opportunity exclusively through the college attainment lens is both strategically ineffective and substantively out of step with the district's material reality." }
