          { name: "Treating 78% as Structural Rather Than Earned", tag: "avoid", why: "A 78% margin in a district with a 27% Black population and 32.6% renter rate is not a natural demographic outcome — it reflects a specific alignment of mobilization, candidate quality, and opposition capacity that can shift. Any incumbent or candidate who campaigns as though 78% is a permanent structural feature of the district, rather than a result requiring active maintenance, is misreading the data. The combination of a large minority community, a financially stressed renter cohort, and a working-class income base gives a credible, well-resourced opponent multiple organizing pathways that a complacent incumbent leaves uncontested. Landslide margins in demographically mixed, income-stressed districts have eroded faster than partisans expect when incumbents stop doing the constituent service work that earned the margin." },
          { name: "Racially Polarizing Rhetoric", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district that is 27.52% Black, any rhetoric that can be read as racially coded — whether on crime, welfare, or 'community values' — will register as a direct message to more than one-in-four constituents that they are not being served. This is not a strategic constraint imposed from outside the district; it is the mathematical reality that a representative governing a 27% Black constituency cannot afford to communicate hostility or indifference to that community without undermining their own capacity to govern effectively and serve all constituents. Beyond governance, racially polarizing messaging activates exactly the mobilization infrastructure that could grow the 22% Democratic share: it gives opposition organizers a concrete, motivating grievance to recruit around in a community large enough to move margins. The 78% is held in part because that organizing infrastructure is currently dormant — antagonistic rhetoric is the fastest way to wake it up." }
