          { name: "Renter Housing Stability & Affordability", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 40.2% renter rate in a district with $40,466 median income is not a preference demographic — it is an economic precarity signal, indicating a large constituency for whom housing costs consume a disproportionate share of household income and for whom eviction, rent increases, and housing quality failures are active governance concerns. In a majority-Black district, renter housing issues also carry a racial equity dimension that is historically legible to this constituency without requiring explicit framing. Specific legislative engagement on tenant protections, housing code enforcement, and affordable housing investment directly addresses the daily material stakes of the 40% of households who do not own and cannot absorb cost shocks the way homeowners can. This is one of the clearest policy-to-constituency alignments in the district's data." },
          { name: "Black Community Crossover Coalition Maintenance", tag: "careful", why: "McElroy's 54% in a 54.61% Black district implies either meaningful Black Republican support or significant Black nonparticipation — and the strategic approach to each is different and potentially contradictory. Pursuing explicit outreach to Black voters risks alienating white Republican base voters who may interpret it as a shift in coalition priorities; ignoring it risks a mobilization cycle where a Democratic challenger consolidates Black turnout and the margin collapses. The 46% Democratic share is the operational evidence that this coalition is not locked in — it is contingent on candidate-specific trust that must be actively renewed. Any strategic move that reads as taking Black voters for granted, or as signaling primarily to the white 40% of the district, should be evaluated against the math: the district is majority-Black and a four-to-five-point shift in Black voter behavior is sufficient to flip the seat." },
          { name: "Abstract Cultural or Ideological Campaigning", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 11.8% of adults hold a college degree and median household income is $40,466, campaign messaging organized around ideological abstraction — culture war framings, national partisan narratives, or policy debates disconnected from daily economic life — lands with a constituency for whom those conversations are a luxury. The electorate in HD-62 is being moved by economic proximity and personal trust, not by ideological sorting, and a candidate who leads with abstract conservative or progressive positioning rather than specific community-level impact will find that approach actively counterproductive in a majority-Black, working-class district where the 54% margin is already narrower than demographics might suggest. Every message that is not about wages, housing, healthcare, or community investment is a missed opportunity to reinforce the coalition that currently holds this seat." },
          { name: "Treating 54% as a Safe or Natural Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 54-46 Republican result in a 54.61% Black, low-income district is one of the most analytically unstable margins in any competitive sequence — it is not a reflection of demographic alignment, it is a candidate-specific performance that could reverse rapidly under changed mobilization conditions. The 46% Democratic share in a district where the demographic baseline should favor Democrats by a wide margin is evidence of Democratic underperformance, not Republican structural strength. Any campaign strategy premised on the assumption that 54% is a floor — that the existing coalition is durable without active maintenance — is arithmetically exposed: a credible Democratic challenger who consolidates Black turnout at anything approaching baseline rates in a 54.61% Black district will produce a different result. This seat requires the strategic posture of a candidate defending a competitive margin, not coasting on incumbency." }
