        memoHeadline: "A Majority-Black Arkansas District Where McElroy Holds 54% With a Fragile Cross-Racial Coalition, Low Educational Attainment, and Real Economic Precarity at $40,466",
        memoParagraphs: [
          "AR-HD-62 is defined by a demographic fact that has no parallel in the comparison districts: at 54.61% Black and 40.01% white, this is a majority-Black constituency whose partisan result — 54% Republican — immediately demands explanation. The McElroy win is not a landslide; it is a working coalition in a district where the demographic majority would, under normal mobilization conditions, produce a Democratic result. That 54-46 margin is sustained by one of two dynamics, or both simultaneously: meaningful crossover support from Black voters who have reasons specific to this candidate, this community, and these economic conditions to break from partisan baseline; or a white minority that votes at high enough rates, with high enough Republican intensity, to anchor the margin while Black turnout or Democratic consolidation falls short. At 11.8% college attainment — among the lowest of any district in any competitive sequence — the electorate is not being persuaded by abstract policy arguments; it is being moved by economic proximity, personal trust, and direct community presence.",
          "The economic profile of HD-62 is the strategic foundation everything else rests on. A $40,466 median household income, 40.2% renter rate, and 42.6 median age together describe a working-age community under sustained financial pressure — not the elderly fixed-income profile of a purely aging rural district, but a prime-working-age constituency that is renting at rates more consistent with economic precarity than with urban lifestyle preference. With only 11.8% college attainment, the district's workforce is concentrated in jobs where wages, healthcare access, and housing costs are the daily material stakes of governance — not abstract legislative priorities. The 46% Democratic opposition in this 54.61% Black district signals that Republican performance here is not demographically natural; it is earned, or borrowed, and must be re-earned each cycle. A candidate who cannot speak credibly to renters facing housing cost pressure, workers without college credentials navigating a constrained wage environment, and a community at $40,466 whose economic margin for error is thin will find that 54% erodes faster than the partisan baseline would suggest."
        ],
        memoBullets: [
          "Treat the 46% Democratic vote as the primary strategic warning: in a 54.61% Black district, that opposition is not a partisan residual — it is a constituency with specific unmet needs around wages, housing stability, and healthcare access that must be identified and directly addressed, not assumed away by incumbency.",
          "Build and maintain authentic, visible presence in Black community institutions — churches, civic organizations, neighborhood networks — because the 54% margin in a majority-Black district is only sustainable if a meaningful portion of Black voters see this candidate as genuinely responsive to their economic reality, not merely tolerated as the lesser option.",
          "Lead on renter-specific economic issues: with 40.2% of the district renting at $40,466 median income, housing affordability, landlord accountability, and utility cost management are the daily financial pressure points that translate directly into whether government is working for the people who actually live here.",
          "Avoid any campaign posture that reads as demographically complacent — a 54-46 result in a majority-Black, low-income district with 11.8% college attainment is not a durable floor; it is a coalition held together by specific trust that can collapse if a credible challenger mobilizes Black voters around economic grievance at higher rates than 2024."
        ]
