          { name: "Economic Mobility and Workforce Investment", tag: "lean-into", why: "A 14.7% college attainment rate and $45,239 median household income together describe a district where most working-age adults are in jobs that do not offer clear upward pathways — and where workforce development, vocational training, wage floors, and access to healthcare are concrete policy levers with direct constituency impact. The 39.3 median age means the bulk of this district is mid-career workers who have already established their economic trajectory but are not yet at retirement age, making them acutely sensitive to whether their earnings are keeping pace with costs. A legislative agenda centered on wage growth, job training infrastructure, and expanding access to employer benefits speaks directly to the material reality of a working-class, majority-Black district. These issues also cross racial lines: the 39.71% white constituency at this income level shares the same workforce mobility constraints, creating a genuine biracial economic coalition opportunity." },
          { name: "Cross-Racial Coalition Maintenance with White Working-Class Voters", tag: "careful", why: "The 39.71% white population is large enough that white voter behavior is a meaningful variable in a district decided at 55-45 — a modest Republican improvement among white working-class voters combined with any softening of Black turnout could compress the margin to a genuinely competitive range. Engaging white working-class constituents on shared economic concerns is both strategically sound and representationally appropriate, but the framing must be grounded in material issues rather than cultural signals that could be read as diluting the district's majority-Black political identity. The risk is appearing to triangulate rather than lead — any candidate perceived as courting white voters at the expense of the Black community that delivers the 55% will face justified credibility challenges. The approach that works is a unified economic security message that speaks honestly to shared conditions without hierarchy among constituencies." },
          { name: "Abstract Ideological Positioning Disconnected from Economic Conditions", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with $45,239 median income, 47.3% renters, and 14.7% college attainment, voters are evaluating candidates on whether they are delivering tangible improvements to material conditions — not on ideological alignment or partisan cultural markers. A campaign or legislative record built primarily around abstract ideological framing, national partisan narratives, or symbolic positioning without specific local policy deliverables is a strategic mismatch with what this constituency is actually asking of its representative. The 45% Republican opposition demonstrates that nearly half the district is already skeptical; giving persuadable voters no concrete economic reason to stay in the coalition while offering only ideological argument is a recipe for margin erosion. HD-63 requires a representative whose case for re-election is built on specific wins for specific people, not on partisan identity alone." },
          { name: "Treating 55% as a Structurally Safe Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 55-45 result in a majority-Black district is a performance outcome, not a demographic entitlement — and the specific conditions that produced it in 2024 must be actively maintained and expanded, not assumed. Black voter turnout in lower-profile state legislative races is more variable than in presidential cycles, and the 39.3 median age means a significant share of the potential electorate includes younger adults whose engagement is not guaranteed. At 47.3% renters, the district has a large mobile population with lower baseline registration and turnout rates than homeowner-majority districts, meaning each cycle requires active mobilization infrastructure rather than reliance on an established voter file. Any candidate who runs HD-63 as though 55% is the floor rather than the ceiling of what active constituent service and community investment can achieve will find the margin shrinking in the first cycle they stop working for it." }
