          { name: "Ignoring the White and Hispanic Minority Communities", tag: "avoid", why: "With 24.55% white and 2.41% Hispanic residents, AR-HD-65 is not exclusively a single-community district — and in a low-income, low-attainment district, white and Hispanic residents at similar income levels share many of the same economic pressures as Black residents around housing, wages, and healthcare access. Framing constituent service exclusively through a racial-majority lens risks both leaving real constituent needs unaddressed and, in a primary context, creating narratives of selective representation that opponents can exploit. The strategic case for broad constituent engagement is not ideological — it is that a representative in a $38,990 median income district is serving economic vulnerability across demographic lines, and doing so visibly strengthens rather than dilutes the case for re-election." }
