          { name: "Economic Mobility, Workforce Development, and Income Growth", tag: "lean-into", why: "A $36,099 median household income combined with 19.8% college attainment and a 35.3 median age describes a young, working-class constituency with significant structural barriers to upward economic mobility. Job training programs, community college funding, apprenticeship pipelines, and small business support are the policy instruments that directly address this constituency's economic trajectory — and a legislator who pursues them with specificity is building a record that resonates with voters who evaluate their representative on whether life is materially improving. The 55.06% Black majority in a district at this income level also means that economic mobility intersects with structural racial equity in ways that are not abstractions but lived daily realities for the majority of constituents." },
          { name: "Hispanic and Residual-Gap Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 11.45% Hispanic and with a small residual demographic gap that may include American Indian, multiracial, or other residents, AR-HD-72 has meaningful constituencies whose specific needs can become invisible when the dominant political and organizing frame centers the majority-Black community. Hispanic renters and workers at this income level face language access barriers, workforce vulnerability, and housing instability that overlap with but are not identical to those of Black constituents. Authentic constituent service for this community — not a single translated mailer, but ongoing engagement — is both a governance obligation and a protection against the fragmentation of a coalition that the incumbent needs cohesive. Neglecting 11-12% of the district in a seat that could face a primary is a structural risk, not a minor omission." },
          { name: "Treating the Unopposed Result as Evidence of Political Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100-0 margin in a district where the opposing party did not field a candidate is not a measure of the incumbent's political strength — it is an artifact of an uncontested race. In majority-Black, high-renter, low-income districts, unopposed status can reflect the opposing party's strategic abandonment rather than constituent satisfaction, and it provides no information about how the electorate would behave in a contested primary. With a 35.3 median age and 63.1% renters, this is a constituency whose turnout and loyalty are sensitive to perceived representation quality, and a challenger who organizes around unmet housing and economic needs can find real traction. Governing as though 100% reflects unconditional support is the fastest path to creating the conditions for a credible primary challenge." },
          { name: "Abstract or Ideologically Framed Messaging Disconnected from Economic Reality", tag: "avoid", why: "A district with $36,099 median income, 63.1% renters, and 19.8% college attainment is a constituency that evaluates politics through the lens of whether government is solving problems they directly face — not through ideological consistency or partisan signaling. Messaging that leads with abstract values, national partisan frames, or demographic-identity appeals without connecting them to specific material improvements in housing, income, and opportunity will read as disconnected from what constituents actually need. The 35.3 median age also means this is a younger electorate with lower inherited partisan attachment — they are persuadable not by loyalty appeals but by demonstrated results, and a communication strategy that does not anchor to specific, concrete deliverables is leaving the district's most persuadable voters unengaged." }
