          { name: "Economic Mobility and Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 16% college attainment rate is the single most constraining structural fact about HD-80's long-term economic trajectory — in a labor market that increasingly premiums credentials, a constituency where only 16 in 100 adults hold a degree faces a structural ceiling on wage growth that $44,511 median income reflects directly. At 38.2 median age, this is a working-age district whose residents are not at the beginning of their careers — they need workforce credentialing, community college access, and trade program investment that delivers economic returns on a working adult's timeline, not a traditional four-year pathway. Connecting workforce development to the district's specific employment sectors and partnering with local employers on credential-to-hire pipelines is the kind of concrete economic agenda that a majority-Black, low-income district with a 41.8% renter rate actually needs from its representative. This is also the issue most likely to generate durable constituent loyalty because its benefits are direct and attributable." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 13.91%, the Hispanic constituency in HD-80 is substantial enough to require specific engagement rather than passive inclusion — but in a district where the 51% Black majority legitimately dominates the political and demographic narrative, the Hispanic community's distinct needs around language-accessible services, workforce conditions, and economic mobility can be effectively invisible. A representative whose outreach, legislative priorities, and community presence are calibrated only to the Black majority is leaving a nearly 14% constituency underserved, which over time creates an opening for a challenger who engages that community specifically. The strategic risk is not neglect born of bad intent but of bandwidth — in a district with acute economic needs across the board, the Hispanic community's concerns require deliberate, dedicated attention rather than assumption that a general economic agenda covers everyone equally." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as Proof of Constituent Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed result reflects candidate recruitment failures, filing barriers, and resource asymmetries as much as it reflects constituent satisfaction — and in a majority-Black, low-income district with a 41.8% renter rate and 16% college attainment, the absence of a challenger in 2024 should not be read as evidence that the constituency's material needs are being met. The conditions in HD-80 are precisely those that generate primary challengers when a community organizer, union leader, or neighborhood advocate concludes the incumbent is not delivering — and at $44,511 income with nearly half the district renting, the economic frustration available to a challenger's organizing message is significant. A representative who campaigns on the strength of the unopposed result rather than on a specific record of housing, workforce, and economic wins is campaigning on the wrong thing entirely." },
          { name: "Abstract Partisan Messaging Disconnected from Economic Specifics", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where $44,511 median income, 41.8% renter rate, and 16% college attainment define the daily material reality for most residents, abstract ideological messaging — whether progressive framing about systemic change or institutional Democratic loyalty appeals — lands as disconnected from the specifics that people in HD-80 actually face. A working-age, majority-Black, renter-majority constituency at this income level evaluates its representative on concrete, attributable results: did housing get more affordable, did wages go up, did the community college get funded, did the eviction moratorium pass. The risk of running on party identity and demographic solidarity rather than on a specific economic record is that it trains the constituency to evaluate the representative on vibes rather than outcomes — which is fine until a primary challenger arrives with a sharper economic critique and a specific list of what did not get done." }
