          { name: "Constituent Service for Black and Hispanic Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 12.27% Black and 11.01% Hispanic communities together represent nearly 23% of the district's population — a substantial minority presence that is currently not reflected in the 32% Democratic vote share in any way that threatens the seat. Responsive, visible constituent service for these communities — on healthcare access, workforce issues, and basic government responsiveness — costs nothing electorally in the short term and builds a reputation for fairness that makes the district harder to organize against. Neglecting nearly a quarter of the district's population is a governance failure that, over multiple cycles, can become a political vulnerability if a challenger successfully frames the incumbent as inaccessible." },
          { name: "Renter Housing Costs and Stability", tag: "careful", why: "The 36.9% renter rate is a meaningful constituency signal in a district that superficially reads as a homeowner-majority community — more than one-in-three households does not benefit from property equity and faces direct exposure to rent increases and housing instability. Engaging renter concerns too aggressively risks friction with the homeowner-majority base and landlord interests that tend to align with Republican coalition donors. But ignoring renters entirely means ignoring a substantial share of the district whose economic anxieties, if activated by a credible challenger, could narrow the 68% margin. The strategic path is constituent service responsiveness rather than legislative crusading — being reachable and helpful to renters without making renter policy a signature issue." },
          { name: "Campaigning on Abstract Culture War Positioning at the Expense of Local Economics", tag: "avoid", why: "At $51,344 income and 25% college attainment, AR-HD-84's working-class majority evaluates their representative primarily through the lens of economic conditions and local responsiveness — not ideological positioning. A candidate who substitutes abstract culture war rhetoric for concrete engagement with healthcare costs, utility rates, and workforce conditions may win the 68% once on partisan loyalty but will find the margin degrading as constituents conclude their material concerns are not being heard. The district is safely Republican on values alignment; it does not need to be won on ideological intensity, and a campaign pitched primarily at that register misreads the constituency." },
          { name: "Treating 68% as a Mandate Requiring No Ongoing Engagement", tag: "avoid", why: "A 68% partisan result in a working-class district with a 23% combined Black and Hispanic population and a 36.9% renter rate is not a permanent structural majority — it is a coalition that holds as long as the minority and renter communities remain unmobilized and the homeowner base remains satisfied. If a well-resourced challenger emerges who can simultaneously activate minority community turnout, speak credibly to renter economic anxiety, and frame the incumbent as inaccessible, the math can shift meaningfully. The 32% base already exists; expanding it by eight to ten points through targeted mobilization is not implausible, and a representative who governs as though the seat is untouchable provides the conditions for exactly that challenge." }
