          { name: "Abstract Culture-War Positioning Disconnected from Economic Reality", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district at $50,278 income with 14.4% college attainment and 27.6% renters, the constituency's primary exposure to government is through its practical economic outcomes — healthcare costs, job availability, housing affordability, and quality of local services. Legislative messaging that prioritizes abstract ideological positioning over these concrete material concerns misreads what a working-class, economically stressed constituency actually needs from its representative. At 14.4% college attainment — the lowest educational attainment signal in this dataset — the district's residents are particularly exposed to economic disruption and particularly dependent on state-level policy getting practical things right. A representative whose public profile is defined by culture-war positioning rather than economic delivery risks a credibility gap that, in a noncompetitive district, may not show up in the partisan margin until it is already too late to address." }
