          "The 29.5% renter rate and $43,230 median household income together describe a constituency under real economic pressure: these are working-class households, many of them renters without the asset cushion of homeownership, navigating rising costs on incomes that leave little margin. The 42.7 median age suggests a community of working-age adults — not retirees, not college students — who are in the middle of the economic challenges that define daily life: housing affordability, healthcare access, job quality, and whether their wages are keeping pace with expenses. The 13.9% college attainment rate is the most significant structural vulnerability in the district's economic profile, because it concentrates the workforce in sectors with lower wages and less job security. A candidate who speaks to workforce training, vocational pathways, and employer investment in non-degree workers is speaking directly to the material reality of roughly 86% of the adults in this district."
