          { name: "Abstract Ideological Positioning Disconnected from Economic Specifics", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where fewer than 1 in 5 adults hold college degrees and median income sits at $55,610, broad ideological framing that does not translate into concrete economic answers for working-class households will land as irrelevant rather than reassuring. The 36.9-year median age and 22.3% renter rate describe a constituency in active economic formation — worried about wages, housing costs, and job security — not a community primarily motivated by abstract political positioning. A legislative record and campaign message built around specific deliverables for non-credentialed workers, renters, and small employers will outperform one built around ideological signals in this district's competitive context. The risk of ideological abstraction is not that it alienates — it is that it fails to give anyone a concrete reason to remain loyal when a challenger offers something tangible." }
