          { name: "Economic Mobility and Workforce Investment for Low-Income Households", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 16.2% college attainment and a $44,629 median household income, HD-100's workforce is concentrated in jobs with limited upward mobility and high exposure to economic volatility — and the district's demographic mix means Black and Hispanic residents are disproportionately positioned in that vulnerable tier. State-level investments in workforce training, community college access, Medicaid coverage for working adults, and small business support for minority entrepreneurs are the policy levers most directly connected to the material concerns of this constituency. A representative who champions these issues with specificity — not generic economic development rhetoric — is addressing what the district actually needs rather than what looks good in a press release. At 37.2 median age, this is also a working-age constituency with decades of economic stake in whether the legislature invests in them." },
          { name: "Black Community Constituent Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 34.78%, Black residents are the district's largest minority community and a constituency whose political engagement in a currently uncontested seat is essentially unmeasured — we have no competitive election data to reveal how they vote, what motivates turnout, or whether they feel represented. The 2024 uncontested result means Dalby has never had to demonstrate cross-racial appeal in a contested environment, which is a significant analytical blind spot. Active, ongoing constituent service and genuine legislative engagement on issues that matter to Black households — economic mobility, healthcare access, criminal justice — is both the right governance posture and the strategic prerequisite for ensuring this community does not become the organizational base for a future opposition campaign. Treating Black constituents as a passive demographic rather than an active civic constituency is the path toward making this seat competitive." },
          { name: "Governing Only for the White Homeowner Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "At 56.54% white and 56.3% homeowner-implied (given the 43.7% renter rate), the district has a white homeowner plurality — but that plurality does not constitute a governing majority when nearly half the district rents and nearly 45% of residents are nonwhite. A legislative record and communication strategy oriented exclusively toward the concerns of white, property-owning constituents leaves a large portion of the district feeling unrepresented, which is precisely the condition that motivates opposition recruitment. In a district where the 2024 result was 100% only because no one filed against Dalby, there is no demonstrated electoral floor — the absence of a challenger is not evidence of a satisfied constituency, and governing exclusively for one demographic cohort tests whether the uncontested result is a floor or a ceiling." },
          { name: "Treating the Uncontested Result as Electoral Proof of District Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 100% result contains zero information about the electorate's actual preferences — it is a measure of filing activity, not constituent sentiment. HD-100's demographic profile, with its 34.78% Black population, 43.7% renter rate, and $44,629 income, closely resembles districts in other Southern states that have flipped when Democratic infrastructure invested in candidate recruitment and voter contact. The strategic error would be to read the uncontested win as evidence that the district's underlying coalition is stable, rather than as evidence that the opposing party has not yet prioritized it. Any planning assumption that treats this seat as structurally safe — rather than contingently uncontested — leaves the incumbent unprepared for the cycle in which a credible challenger does file, with a well-defined constituency already primed by unmet economic needs." }
