          { name: "Culture-War Positioning as Primary Legislative Identity", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district at $48,700 median income with 17.8% college attainment, the daily economic pressures on most households are immediate enough that a representative whose primary public identity is cultural-conflict signaling rather than economic delivery will eventually be held accountable for the gap between rhetorical priorities and material results. The 77% margin creates the temptation to perform ideological positioning for a state or national audience rather than doing the specific constituent work that a working-class, racially mixed district actually needs. A legislator who becomes known primarily for culture-war engagement rather than for economic development, healthcare access, or infrastructure investment in AR-SD-1 is choosing visibility in a national media narrative over durability in the district — a trade-off that safe-seat incumbents make more often than they should." }
