          { name: "Abstract Partisan Messaging Without Local Grounding", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where 77% of adults lack a four-year degree and the median household earns $68,383, broad ideological messaging disconnected from kitchen-table economic specifics does not build durable constituent loyalty — it produces voter indifference, which is the precondition for low-turnout upsets when a challenger eventually files. The 23% college attainment figure is the most important number in the district for messaging strategy: it tells a communicator that abstract policy framing, partisan identity appeals, and credentialed-professional-coded language will underperform relative to direct, specific, impact-oriented communication about what the legislator has done for this community. Candidates in AR-SD-7 who lead with partisan identity rather than economic delivery are optimizing for a metric — partisan alignment — that is already maxed out in an unopposed seat, rather than the metric that actually matters: constituent trust earned through tangible results." }
