          { name: "Black Community Constituent Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 11.38% of the district's population, Black residents represent the largest non-white constituency in AR-SD-11 and a community whose electoral behavior has never been revealed by a contested race — the 2022 unopposed result tells us nothing about their preferences, mobilization capacity, or satisfaction with current representation. In a district where the combined non-white population approaches 19%, genuine, sustained engagement with Black community institutions, concerns around economic mobility, and healthcare access is both a governance obligation and a latent electoral variable that any challenger would immediately attempt to activate. An incumbent who has built authentic relationships here before a competitive cycle is far more durable than one who has assumed the community's acquiescence from an absence of opposition." },
          { name: "Renter and Housing Affordability Constituency", tag: "careful", why: "The 30.9% renter rate in AR-SD-11 is a significant signal in a state legislative district — nearly a third of households have no homeownership stake and face a distinct set of economic pressures around rent levels, housing availability, and landlord-tenant dynamics that homeowner-majority messaging does not address. This constituency skews younger given the 36.3 median age and is likely concentrated among the district's working-class and minority residents, creating an overlap between the renter community and the Black and Hispanic constituencies who together represent about 17% of the population. Addressing renter concerns requires careful framing that does not alienate the homeowner majority while still demonstrating that the district's 31% renting households have a credible legislative advocate — getting the balance wrong in either direction leaves votes on the table." },
          { name: "Assuming the Unopposed Result Reflects True Support", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 100% result is the least informative electoral outcome possible — it reveals turnout mechanics, not preference, and a candidate who campaigns the next cycle as though the 2022 result constitutes a demonstrated mandate is making a strategically dangerous inference. Every percentage point of latent opposition that went unmeasured in 2022 is a percentage point that a credible challenger can organize, and in a district with a 30.9% renter rate, 11.38% Black population, and a $69,163 working-class income, the structural conditions for a competitive race exist even if recent ballot history does not show it. Treating this seat as safe rather than as an untested seat is the single most reliable path to being surprised by a competitive margin when competition arrives." },
          { name: "Ignoring the ~5% AIAN Demographic Gap", tag: "avoid", why: "The four reported Census categories in AR-SD-11 sum to approximately 95%, leaving a ~5% gap that in Arkansas most likely represents American Indian and Alaska Native residents — a smaller signal than in Oklahoma's tribal-territory districts but one that still represents real constituents with specific governance interests. While this community is not the dominant swing factor it is in some neighboring-state districts, a candidate who gives zero attention to Native American constituent concerns in a district where they plausibly represent several percentage points of the population is leaving a small but real constituency entirely unserved. In a district that has never been tested by competition, even marginal constituencies can matter when every available vote suddenly counts." }
