          { name: "Economic Development and Workforce Investment in Majority-Black Communities", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 54.18% Black and $41,924 median income with only 22.5% college attainment, AR-SD-12 is a district where the gap between economic potential and economic outcome is the central governance challenge. Workforce development programs, job training pipelines, small business access to capital, and targeted economic investment in the communities that make up the district's demographic majority are the policy instruments that translate representation into material improvement. A legislator's record on these specifics is the accountability measure that matters most when the district has no partisan competition to generate external pressure. The 36.3 median age signals a working-age constituency that is still in the process of building economic security — the policy window to affect those outcomes is now." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 8.44%, the Hispanic community in AR-SD-12 is a meaningful constituency that can easily become invisible in a district where the dominant demographic narrative centers on the Black-white composition. Healthcare access, workforce conditions, language accessibility in government services, and economic mobility concerns for the Hispanic community are real and present needs that deserve specific rather than aggregate attention. In an unopposed seat, underserving the 8.44% carries no immediate electoral cost — but it creates the conditions for organized dissatisfaction that a future challenger can activate. Proactive engagement here is low-cost constituency investment with asymmetric upside." },
          { name: "Treating the Unopposed Result as a Performance Review", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% unopposed result reflects ballot access and filing dynamics — it is not a constituent satisfaction score, a job approval rating, or a measure of legislative effectiveness. At $41,924 income and 54.8% renter rate, the district's constituents are living with material economic pressures that have nothing to do with whether someone filed to run against the incumbent. Any public framing or internal strategic posture that interprets the uncontested result as evidence of strong performance rather than an absence of organized opposition is a misread that produces complacency exactly where urgency is warranted. The district's demographics and economic conditions guarantee that unmet needs exist at scale." },
          { name: "Governing Without a Visible Presence in the District", tag: "avoid", why: "In majority-Black, majority-renter, lower-income districts, the most common source of primary vulnerability is not policy disagreement but perceived absence — constituents who feel their senator is not present, not accessible, and not fighting visibly on their behalf. At 22.5% college attainment, the district's constituency communicates and evaluates through community presence, local institutions, and direct service rather than through legislative scorecards or media coverage. A representative who is not consistently visible in the churches, community organizations, neighborhood associations, and local institutions of a 54.18% Black district at $41,924 income is allowing the primary challenger's opening to build quietly, regardless of what the legislative record actually shows." }
