          { name: "Middle-Income Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The $77,425 median household income and implied ~72% homeownership rate describe a constituency that is economically established but not insulated from financial pressure — the cohort most likely to be the persuadable center in a 52-48 district. At 40.5 median age, these voters are in peak earning and homeownership years but carry real exposure to healthcare costs, property tax increases, and wage stagnation. Concrete commitments on property tax fairness, healthcare affordability, and economic development speak directly to the anxieties of this demographic without requiring ideological conversion. In a four-point race, owning this agenda credibly is the difference between consolidating the existing 48% and adding the margin needed to win." },
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Voters", tag: "careful", why: "At 38.7% college attainment — meaningfully above the Arkansas state average — SD-13 has a professional class large enough to matter in a district where four points determines the outcome, but this constituency does not vote as a monolith and can be alienated by messaging that feels condescending or ideologically charged in either direction. The overlap between this group and both the Black community's upwardly mobile households and white middle-income homeowners means that workforce, education, and economic competitiveness messaging can serve multiple constituencies simultaneously — but only if it is framed around practical outcomes rather than credential-based cultural signaling. Engage on issues like healthcare system quality, economic development, and public education in ways that resonate across the attainment spectrum rather than pitching narrowly to the college-educated as a distinct tribe." },
          { name: "Treating 52% as a Stable Floor", tag: "avoid", why: "English's 52% in 2022 is not a majority — it is a four-point plurality in a district where 48% of voters actively chose the other candidate. Campaigning this seat as though the Republican lean is a structural advantage miscounts the evidence: a quarter of the district is African American, nearly 30% are renters, and the Democratic baseline is already within a single persuasion cycle of winning. Any incumbent or challenger who consolidates only the existing Republican coalition and neglects the specific economic and community concerns that generated 48% opposition will find SD-13 flipped in the next cycle with a strong Democratic candidate or above-average Black turnout. The 52% result demands active constituent management, not quiet confidence." },
          { name: "Racially Polarizing Rhetoric or Coded Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district that is 25.48% Black and decided by four points, any messaging that activates racial resentment or deploys coded language about crime, welfare, or urban dysfunction is arithmetically self-defeating — it will consolidate Black voters against the candidate and push away the crossover white voters whose 2022 defection from the Republican baseline produced the competitive 48% in the first place. The persuadable middle in SD-13 is economically anxious, not ideologically extreme, and responds to material concerns rather than cultural warfare framing. A candidate who goes negative on racial or cultural grounds is trading the slim margin they need for turnout among a base that is already voting for them." }
