          { name: "Economic Mobility and Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 26% college attainment rate, combined with a $46,766 median income and a young district median age of 32.8, describes a workforce that is largely employed in sectors with limited upward mobility and high income volatility. Job training, community college access, apprenticeship pathways, and living wage advocacy are the policy levers most directly relevant to the economic trajectory of residents in this district. For the 56.5% Black community and 18.2% Hispanic community — both of which face documented structural barriers to wage growth and asset accumulation — workforce development is not a generic economic issue but a racial and economic justice priority with direct constituent impact. A legislator who advances specific, funded workforce programs can point to tangible outcomes that sustain the coalition's engagement." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Constituent Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "At 18.2% of the district, the Hispanic community is substantial enough that its turnout and satisfaction materially affect the overall Democratic margin — lower Hispanic participation in a high-turnout district still moves aggregate numbers at this scale. Hispanic residents in Arkansas face distinct challenges around language access in government services, workforce sector vulnerabilities in agriculture and construction, and in some cases immigration-status complexity that affects healthcare and benefit access. A representative who addresses these concerns through language-accessible outreach, specific policy attention to labor and housing issues affecting Hispanic workers, and visible community presence will maintain the coalition's depth more effectively than one who treats the 76% combined minority majority as a monolithic bloc. The careful dimension is ensuring this engagement is substantive and consistent, not performative, because a community that perceives tokenism disengages rather than defects — which in a high-turnout district means margin erosion that looks invisible until election night." },
          { name: "Treating 86% as a Mandate Rather Than a Responsibility", tag: "avoid", why: "An 86% margin is produced by a constituency that has invested near-total political trust in a representative — it is not a free pass to govern at a distance. In a district defined by economic precarity at $46,766 median income, near-majority renter status, and a young population with limited asset accumulation, unmet constituent needs do not produce partisan defection; they produce disengagement, declining turnout, and a slow erosion of the coalition that built the margin. A representative who interprets 86% as electoral security rather than as a governing obligation will find that margin compressing not because Republicans gained ground but because the coalition's most economically stressed members stopped showing up. The strategic risk in AR-SD-15 is not losing — it is winning 72% in a year when it matters." },
          { name: "Neglecting the Young Renter Majority in Favor of Visible Base Activation", tag: "avoid", why: "At 32.8 years median age and 48.3% renters, AR-SD-15's electoral coalition is disproportionately composed of young adults who are not yet property owners and whose relationship to politics is mediated by whether government is visibly improving their material conditions. Campaign and constituent strategies that prioritize high-visibility institutional relationships — established churches, legacy civic organizations, older homeowner networks — while underserving the district's young renter majority risk a quiet but consequential turnout gap in this precise demographic. Young renters at $46,766 median income do not have the economic buffer or civic habit formation that makes them reliable off-cycle voters absent specific engagement; taking their participation for granted because they voted at 86% in 2022 is a strategically costly assumption." }
