          { name: "Abstract Culture-War Messaging Over Material Economic Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with $53,242 median income, 15.4% college attainment, and a 36.5% renter rate, the constituency's primary orientation is toward concrete economic conditions — whether wages are rising, whether healthcare is accessible, whether the roads and utilities work. Messaging that leads with abstract ideological or culture-war themes rather than specific economic and infrastructure priorities risks alienating the working-class white majority whose loyalty is conditional on feeling economically represented, while doing nothing to build relationship with the Black and Hispanic communities whose economic concerns are even more acute. At 18.64% Black and 4.25% Hispanic, this district's minority communities have specific, material needs that abstract partisan positioning actively fails to address. A candidate whose message centers economic specificity — jobs, healthcare, housing costs — performs across coalition lines that ideological messaging cannot reach." }
