          { name: "Rural Economic Security and Cost-of-Living Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $53,547 median household income with 29.5% of residents renting — not the profile of an affluent district insulated from economic stress — AR-SD-25 has a working-class constituency for whom healthcare costs, utility rates, and housing affordability are concrete daily concerns. A legislative record focused on rural hospital viability, prescription drug costs, and practical infrastructure investment speaks directly to the material reality of a district at this income level. The 74% margin reflects partisan alignment, not satisfaction with economic conditions, and the gap between those two things is where a credible challenger eventually finds oxygen. Owning this issue with specific legislative action is the most durable way to hold the coalition." },
          { name: "Workforce Development and Vocational Training", tag: "lean-into", why: "With only 22.4% college attainment, AR-SD-25 is a working-class district where the relevant economic mobility pathway is vocational training, apprenticeship, and skilled trades — not four-year university pipelines. A legislative agenda that funds community college technical programs, expands apprenticeship access, and addresses the rural workforce pipeline speaks directly to what this constituency needs and what parents in a 37.8-median-age district are thinking about for their children. This is both a governance priority and a political differentiator: a senator who can point to specific workforce investments in the district creates a tangible record that abstract partisan positioning cannot match. At $53,547 median income, job quality and skills access are the economic issues that move households." },
          { name: "Hispanic Community Constituent Service", tag: "careful", why: "The 10.96% Hispanic population in AR-SD-25 is large enough — roughly one in nine residents — to have distinct and specific constituent needs that a legislator focused exclusively on the Anglo homeowner majority will systematically under-serve. In Arkansas's rural districts, Hispanic residents are disproportionately concentrated in agricultural and food-processing industries where labor conditions, wage enforcement, and healthcare access are direct quality-of-life concerns at below-median household income. Proactive constituent service, Spanish-language outreach, and specific attention to workforce and healthcare issues for this community builds goodwill without threatening the 74% base — but neglect creates a latent grievance that a future challenger can mobilize. The careful framing is warranted because engagement must be substantive, not performative: tokenism in a 74-point district is both unnecessary and counterproductive." },
          { name: "Treating the 74% Margin as an Indefinite Safe Harbor", tag: "avoid", why: "A 74-26 result in 2024 reflects the current alignment of a working-class, predominantly white rural constituency with the Republican brand — it does not reflect unconditional personal loyalty to any specific incumbent. At $53,547 median income with 29.5% renters and a Hispanic community approaching 11%, the district contains constituencies with specific unmet needs that accumulate into primary vulnerabilities if ignored over multiple legislative cycles. Arkansas Republican primaries have repeatedly punished incumbents perceived as insufficiently attentive to local district concerns, regardless of their ideological positioning. Governing this district as though 74% is a floor rather than a snapshot is the most reliable way to erode it." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Renter Constituency as a Non-Factor", tag: "avoid", why: "With 29.5% of AR-SD-25 residents renting — nearly one in three households — treating housing policy as irrelevant to this district's concerns is a material misreading of who lives here. At $53,547 median income, renters in this district are not urban professionals choosing flexibility; they are working-class households for whom rental cost increases directly compress household budgets and for whom a policy environment that ignores tenant-side housing pressure creates real economic harm. A legislative record with no attention to housing cost, rental market conditions, or mobile home park resident protections — a significant renter category in rural Arkansas — signals to this third of the constituency that their specific economic situation is invisible to their senator. In a primary context, that invisibility is a recruiting poster for a challenger." }
