          { name: "Treating the 64% Margin as a Governing Mandate Rather Than a Diagnostic Puzzle", tag: "avoid", why: "A 64-36 Republican result in a district that is approaching 40% non-white with 48.4% renters and a 36.2 median age is not a stable reflection of constituent preferences — it is a snapshot of a turnout and mobilization imbalance. Any incumbent who interprets this margin as permission to govern without sustained engagement of Hispanic, Black, Asian, and AIAN constituents is misreading the district's structural composition. The demographic trajectory of AR-SD-27 runs toward greater diversity and greater renter concentration, meaning the coalition that produced 64% in 2022 faces headwinds in future cycles if minority-community engagement and turnout infrastructure are allowed to develop on the opposition side." },
          { name: "College-Credential and Professional-Class Messaging as the District's Primary Frame", tag: "avoid", why: "At 24.9% college attainment, AR-SD-27 is a working-class district where the large majority of voters — across all racial groups — did not complete a four-year degree. Campaign messaging built around credentialism, professional-class policy vocabulary, or frameworks that implicitly valorize higher education as the path to economic success will alienate the district's core constituency. The district's economic concerns are immediate and material — rent, wages, healthcare costs, job stability — and a candidate who frames solutions in language that resonates primarily with the 24.9% college-educated minority is actively narrowing their appeal in a district where the other 75% are the strategic majority." }
