          { name: "Rural Healthcare & Medicare Sustainability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 44.2 median age and $53,101 household income, AR-SD-28 is a district of older, income-constrained households for whom healthcare costs and rural hospital access are immediate material concerns — not abstract policy questions. Rural hospital closures have accelerated across Arkansas, and a district at this income and age intersection is acutely exposed to the consequences of reduced rural healthcare infrastructure. A legislator who can credibly claim to have protected or expanded local healthcare access — whether through rural hospital funding, Medicaid policy, or Medicare advocacy — is delivering the single most tangible economic security benefit this constituency needs. The 17.8% college attainment rate means this message must be communicated in concrete, personal terms: what facility, what service, what cost is being protected." },
          { name: "Agricultural Economy & Rural Infrastructure", tag: "lean-into", why: "Rural Arkansas Senate districts at this income level are structurally tied to agricultural employment, supply chains, and the rural infrastructure — roads, broadband, water systems — that makes farm-dependent communities viable. At $53,101 median income with low educational attainment, the workforce here does not have a clear pathway out of agricultural and resource-dependent employment, which means the health of that economic sector is the health of the district. Specific engagement on farm credit access, crop insurance, rural broadband expansion, and agricultural workforce issues speaks directly to the economic reality of constituents who have limited alternatives to the land-based economy. A legislator who demonstrates knowledge of and advocacy for the specific agricultural conditions in this part of Arkansas — not generic rural rhetoric — builds the kind of credibility that 76% margins require to remain stable." },
          { name: "AIAN Community Constituent Service", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported demographic categories sum to approximately 90%, leaving a ~10% gap that in rural Arkansas almost certainly represents American Indian or Alaska Native residents — a community whose electoral weight is small but whose constituent needs are real and often underserved. At the district's $53,101 income level and with limited local institutional resources, AIAN residents face compounding barriers to healthcare access, economic mobility, and government responsiveness that a legislator can address through specific constituent service attention. This community does not require special political handling, but treating their absence from the electoral margin as license to ignore their presence as constituents is both a governance failure and an unnecessary vulnerability. Active, visible responsiveness to AIAN constituent needs costs nothing electorally and builds authentic community credibility." },
          { name: "Abstract Ideological Positioning Disconnected from Economic Deliverables", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with 17.8% college attainment and $53,101 median income, legislative communication anchored in ideological abstraction — whether cultural grievance politics or policy framework arguments — will underperform against a challenger who speaks directly to hospital closures, farm debt, or job loss. The 76% result reflects structural partisan alignment, not an ideologically engaged electorate that rewards legislative philosophy. A primary challenger who presents as more aggressively populist on economic grievances — not more ideologically sophisticated — is the actual threat to this seat. Every legislative communication that cannot be translated into 'here is what I did for your hospital, your farm, your paycheck' is a missed opportunity to build the concrete credibility that protects a 76% margin from erosion." },
          { name: "Treating 76% as an Invitation to Disengage", tag: "avoid", why: "A 76-24 result in a structurally aligned district is not evidence of personal popularity — it is evidence of a strong partisan floor, and floors can collapse when a primary challenger activates a dormant constituency or when a visible constituent service failure disrupts the implicit compact between the legislator and the community. At 44.2 median age and with a constituency heavily dependent on healthcare and agricultural systems that are under structural pressure statewide, the conditions for a credible 'you aren't delivering' primary challenge are latent and real. The 26.4% renter rate also signals a minority of younger, more economically precarious households whose needs can become a wedge issue if visibly neglected. Infrequent town halls, low constituent responsiveness, or a legislative record that cannot point to specific local wins are the operational vulnerabilities that convert a 76% seat into a 55% primary survival." }
