          { name: "Rural Economic Stability and Cost-of-Living Pressures", tag: "lean-into", why: "At $63,114 median household income and 22.0% college attainment, AR-SD-29 is a working-class-to-modest-middle-class district where the gap between earnings and rising fixed costs — groceries, fuel, utilities, insurance — is a lived daily reality rather than a political talking point. The 40.0 median age means the dominant cohort is working-age adults with families and mortgages, not retirees on fixed income or young renters with flexibility. Specific legislative action on property tax relief, agricultural input costs, and small business regulatory burden translates directly into constituent benefit for this demographic. A legislator who can name specific bills, specific dollar amounts, and specific local impacts has a durable advantage over any challenger who can only offer abstract promises." },
          { name: "Rural Healthcare Access", tag: "lean-into", why: "Rural Arkansas consistently faces hospital closures, physician shortages, and emergency service gaps, and at 40.0 median age with 22.7% renters — suggesting a mix of families and working adults with limited geographic mobility — healthcare access is not a peripheral concern for AR-SD-29 constituents. A legislator who can point to specific rural hospital funding, telemedicine expansion, or workforce recruitment wins in this district earns credibility that no partisan positioning can replicate. The ~10% AIAN population, likely spread across rural areas with the least healthcare infrastructure, amplifies this issue's reach. Healthcare is also the issue most likely to activate a previously dormant opposition in a district currently held without contest — owning it proactively is the stronger strategic position." },
          { name: "American Indian and Alaska Native Constituent Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported demographic categories sum to roughly 90%, leaving a ~10% gap that in rural Arkansas almost certainly reflects AIAN residents — a community that does not show up prominently in partisan results but whose political mobilization, when organized around specific local grievances, can shift competitive margins significantly. At current margins this constituency has no leverage, but that is a function of dormancy rather than indifference. A legislator who proactively engages tribal communities, attends to specific concerns around recognition, land, and healthcare access, and builds authentic relationships earns loyalty that functions as insurance against future competitive cycles. Ignoring this community because it currently presents no electoral threat is the kind of oversight that creates future vulnerabilities." },
          { name: "Governing as Though 100% Equals 100% Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2024 result of 100% Republican reflects an uncontested race, not a referendum on the incumbent's record — and treating it as the latter is a strategic error that produces overconfidence and underinvestment in constituent relationships. A district at $63,114 income with real rural service pressures has constituents with real grievances that simply had no electoral outlet in 2024; those grievances do not disappear because no one filed to oppose them. Any candidate whose public posture conveys satisfaction with the current state of the district's services, infrastructure, or economic conditions risks appearing out of touch to a constituency that knows firsthand what is and is not working. The 100% result is an asset to protect through engagement, not a mandate to coast on." },
          { name: "Abstract Ideological Positioning Disconnected from District Economics", tag: "avoid", why: "With 22.0% college attainment and a median income of $63,114 in a rural district, AR-SD-29's voters evaluate their representative primarily on whether government is delivering tangible results — not on ideological purity scores or national culture-war alignments. A legislative record dominated by symbolic bills or national-partisan messaging at the expense of concrete district-level deliverables on healthcare, infrastructure, agriculture, or local economic development leaves the incumbent exposed to a primary challenger who can credibly claim to fight harder on the same values while doing more for actual constituents. In a district never tested by general-election competition, the primary is the real threat, and primary voters in rural Arkansas are not rewarded by abstraction — they want results they can see and name." }
