          { name: "The ~17% Unaccounted Demographic Gap", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported categories — white (39.52%), Hispanic/Latino (38.92%), Black (2.58%), Asian (2.34%) — sum to approximately 83%, leaving a ~17% gap that in Arkansas likely represents a specific community requiring identification before electoral assumptions are finalized. Depending on the district's precise geography, this could include Marshallese and other Pacific Islander communities concentrated in northwest Arkansas, Native American residents, or multiracial households — each of which has distinct civic infrastructure, political concerns, and mobilization patterns. At roughly 17% of the population, this is not a rounding error; it could represent the margin between 60% and a closer result, and any candidate who has not specifically engaged this community's institutions and concerns is operating with an incomplete map of the electorate." },
          { name: "Treating the 60% Margin as Structural Rather Than Earned", tag: "avoid", why: "A 60-40 result in a district that is 38.92% Hispanic/Latino, 46.7% renter, and 33.2 years median age is demographically counterintuitive — it reflects a specific 2022 candidate and context, not a stable structural majority. Any campaign that assumes the 60% is a reliable floor rather than a performance to be re-earned will be caught off-guard if Hispanic voter participation increases, if renter economic frustration crystallizes around a specific issue, or if a credible challenger runs a targeted economic message at the young adult majority. The 40% is not an inert losing coalition; it is an active constituency whose organizing potential in this demographic environment is significant." },
          { name: "Ideological Messaging Divorced from Economic Specifics", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district with a 33.2-year median age, 46.7% renter rate, and near-plurality Hispanic population, abstract ideological framing — whether partisan identity politics or cultural positioning — underperforms against concrete economic specificity. Young adults and renters at $65,672 median income evaluate political candidates primarily on whether they understand the material pressures of building a life: housing costs, healthcare, wages, and job quality. A campaign that leads with ideological signaling rather than demonstrable knowledge of these economic conditions will fail to hold the persuadable middle of a district whose 40% opposition is demographically positioned to grow if given a compelling alternative." }
